WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.240
During a time before there were clocks, a slave boy named Jang Youngsil was born.

2
00:00:06.241 --> 00:00:10.258
Even though Youngsil was a slave, he was very talented and bright.

3
00:00:10.259 --> 00:00:14.692
He could fix anything, and even better, he made fantastic inventions.

4
00:00:14.693 --> 00:00:24.323
By the time he was ten years old, so many people had heard of his talent that the government office of Busan decided to hire him.

5
00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:27.352
One summer there was a terrible drought in Busan.

6
00:00:27.353 --> 00:00:33.543
Youngsil thought to himself, "If only there was a way to get water from the mountains to the fields!"

7
00:00:33.544 --> 00:00:36.923
Suddenly, he had an idea. That was it!

8
00:00:36.924 --> 00:00:41.607
He could make a long pipe that would direct the water from the mountain streams to the farms!

9
00:00:41.608 --> 00:00:47.904
Everyone was amazed. Youngsil had invented an irrigation pipe.

10
00:00:47.905 --> 00:00:51.552
King Sejong heard of Youngsil's amazing pipe.

11
00:00:51.553 --> 00:00:55.311
"This boy must be a genius!" the king thought.

12
00:00:55.312 --> 00:00:59.884
"I must meet him!" King Sejong summoned Youngsil to court.

13
00:00:59.885 --> 00:01:03.276
When the king met Youngsil, he saw great potential.

14
00:01:03.277 --> 00:01:07.601
"But first he must learn more," the king said to himself.

15
00:01:07.602 --> 00:01:14.493
The king asked the great astrologer Yoon Saung to be Youngsil's mentor.

16
00:01:14.494 --> 00:01:19.669
When the king saw that Youngsil was a quick learner, he sent him to China to study astrology.

17
00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:25.346
Youngsil studied how the Chinese built water clocks, astrolabes, and sundials.

18
00:01:25.347 --> 00:01:30.288
He was amazed at the advances in science that the Chinese had made.

19
00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:37.740
"I must use what I have learned here to help advance Korean science," Youngsil thought.

20
00:01:37.741 --> 00:01:44.242
When Youngsil returned from China, the king told his state ministers that he wished to give Youngsil a government position.

21
00:01:44.243 --> 00:01:51.757
The ministers protested and cried out, "But Your Majesty, a slave cannot take a government position!"

22
00:01:51.758 --> 00:01:59.115
Hwang Hui, the minister of culture and education, stepped forward and said, "Talent is more important than social status.

23
00:01:59.116 --> 00:02:03.325
Youngsil deserves this honor!"

24
00:02:03.326 --> 00:02:06.265
The ministers saw the truth in Hwang Hui's statement.

25
00:02:06.266 --> 00:02:10.027
In 1423, Youngsil became a government official.

26
00:02:10.028 --> 00:02:13.710
Youngsil vowed to use this honor to help his people and his king.

27
00:02:13.711 --> 00:02:15.475
He decided to make a clock.

28
00:02:15.476 --> 00:02:18.327
A clock would help farmers know when to plant crops.

29
00:02:18.328 --> 00:02:22.903
A clock would make daily life more organized.

30
00:02:22.904 --> 00:02:28.104
Youngsil modeled his clock on Chinese water clocks and presented it to King Sejong.

31
00:02:28.105 --> 00:02:34.065
After the king saw Youngsil's marvelous water clock, he commissioned Youngsil to invent an astrolabe.

32
00:02:34.066 --> 00:02:36.828
A great invention often requires teamwork.

33
00:02:36.829 --> 00:02:43.110
So, fellow government official Yi Chun worked with Youngsil on the astrolabe.

34
00:02:43.111 --> 00:02:44.608
After careful research,

35
00:02:44.609 --> 00:02:52.674
they constructed a sphere that enabled a person to measure the angle between a planet or star and the point directly over that person's head.

36
00:02:52.675 --> 00:03:00.805
By measuring that angle, scientists could find out precisely where they were located in relation to the rest of the universe.

37
00:03:00.806 --> 00:03:02.624
Youngsil was very happy.

38
00:03:02.625 --> 00:03:06.470
Now people could tell time and study the stars and solar system.

39
00:03:06.471 --> 00:03:10.330
However, he was not satisfied with his water clock.

40
00:03:10.331 --> 00:03:15.522
"How could I create a clock that would tell time automatically?" he wondered.

41
00:03:15.523 --> 00:03:19.421
Youngsil studied automatic clocks invented in China and Arabia.

42
00:03:19.422 --> 00:03:27.166
After careful research he created an automatic water clock in 1434.

43
00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:31.830
The clock worked by letting water drip from a large container into a small container.

44
00:03:31.831 --> 00:03:38.616
When the small container was full, it pushed a small marble into a big marble, which then caused a bell to ring.

45
00:03:38.617 --> 00:03:45.221
The king was so pleased with this automatic water clock that he promoted Youngsil to an even higher government position.

46
00:03:45.222 --> 00:03:50.303
This was a great honor, indeed!

47
00:03:50.304 --> 00:03:57.556
When Youngsil saw the farmers working hard to feed everyone, he decided to invent a device that would predict floods and droughts.

48
00:03:57.557 --> 00:04:01.430
A jar filled with rainwater gave him a brilliant idea.

49
00:04:01.431 --> 00:04:06.046
He would make a container that would collect the rain and then measure how much had fallen!

50
00:04:06.047 --> 00:04:13.062
Thus, in 1441, Youngsil invented the world's first rain gauge.

51
00:04:13.063 --> 00:04:15.986
Youngsil was proud of everything he had invented.

52
00:04:15.987 --> 00:04:20.831
But he knew that his success was because the king had given him a government position.

53
00:04:20.832 --> 00:04:24.313
So, he decided to thank the king by making him a carriage.

54
00:04:24.314 --> 00:04:28.238
When King Sejong rode in Youngsil's carriage, however, it broke!

55
00:04:28.239 --> 00:04:35.594
Youngsil was sent to prison for making a poorly built carriage and was never heard from again.

56
00:04:35.595 --> 00:04:39.741
Some say the king, who believed in Youngsil, secretly released him.

57
00:04:39.742 --> 00:04:43.046
Some say that Youngsil used his genius to escape.

58
00:04:43.047 --> 00:04:48.078
Others say he died in prison. No one will ever know what became of Youngsil.

59
00:04:48.079 --> 00:04:50.961
Yet we know what became of his inventions.

60
00:04:50.962 --> 00:04:53.731
They contributed greatly to Korean science.

61
00:04:53.732 --> 00:05:00.207
Even today, the Jang Youngsil science award rewards talented Korean scientists.

62
00:05:00.208 --> 00:05:03.754
Youngsil's many inventions changed the lives of Koreans.

63
00:05:03.755 --> 00:05:07.416
Thanks to him, Koreans could farm well and tell time.

64
00:05:07.417 --> 00:05:11.490
He was born a slave at a time when status was more important than talent.

65
00:05:11.491 --> 00:05:15.972
But Jang Youngsil showed that talent is more important than status.

66
00:05:15.973 --> 00:05:21.528
He is proof that hard work can lead to great success.

